SDL2 window port -- build notes
================================

This is a scaffold: it gives UnNetHack a real graphical window and a real
event loop. The map draws real tile art (tilesets/unchozo32b.png) via the
same glyph2tile[] mapping the X11/Qt ports use; message/status/menu text
renders via SDL2_ttf. If no usable tileset is found at startup, the map
falls back to a single colored character per glyph instead of failing --
see "Known gaps" below.

Dependencies
------------
  - SDL2
  - SDL2_ttf
  - SDL2_image (built with PNG support -- needed to load
    tilesets/unchozo32b.png)
  - a monospace TrueType font on the build machine (the port tries a
    short list of common macOS/Linux paths at startup; see
    sdl_font_candidates in win/sdl/sdlmain.c if yours isn't found)
  - tilesets/unchozo32b.png present in HACKDIR at runtime (see "Tileset
    placement" below) -- it's the only bundled sheet in tilesets/ with
    enough tiles (1680) to cover this fork's total_tiles_used (1676);
    the others (unh16.bmp, unh32.bmp, untiles32x.png) fall short and
    are silently skipped by sdl_tile_candidates' search even if present

macOS (Homebrew):
    brew install sdl2 sdl2_ttf sdl2_image

Debian/Ubuntu:
    apt install libsdl2-dev libsdl2-ttf-dev libsdl2-image-dev

Enabling the port
------------------
This project builds via autoconf (configure/config.status), not the
classic hand-edited sys/unix/Makefile.src workflow -- sys/autoconf/Makefile.src
is the real template (sys/unix/Makefile.src is kept in sync for anyone
still using the older manual build, but isn't what `configure` reads).
There's no `--enable-sdl-graphics` configure option yet, so enabling the
port today means:

1. In include/config.h, uncomment:
       #define SDL_GRAPHICS

   Leave whatever other *_GRAPHICS port you already use defined too if
   you want both available at runtime (selectable via the `windowtype`
   option, e.g. `NETHACKOPTIONS="windowtype:sdl"` or `windowtype:sdl`
   in .unnethackrc) -- all compiled-in ports coexist in one binary.

2. Whichever Makefile.src your build actually uses (usually the
   autoconf-generated src/Makefile -- check with
   `grep '^WINSRC' src/Makefile`), add sdl to the active selection
   rather than replacing it, e.g.:
       WINSRC = $(WINTTYSRC) $(WINCURSESSRC) $(WINSDLSRC)
       WINOBJ = $(WINTTYOBJ) $(WINCURSESOBJ) $(WINSDLOBJ)
       WINLIB = $(WINTTYLIB) $(WINCURSESLIB) $(WINSDLLIB)
   and make sure CFLAGS/LFLAGS can find SDL2/SDL2_ttf/SDL2_image (on
   Apple Silicon Homebrew: `-I/opt/homebrew/include`,
   `-L/opt/homebrew/lib`).

3. Rebuild (`cd src && make`), then `make install` from the repo root
   to stage the binary -- this does NOT yet copy tilesets/unchozo32b.png
   into the install target, so see "Tileset placement" below.

Tileset placement
------------------
sdl_init_nhwindows() looks for the tileset relative to the current
directory, which is HACKDIR by the time it runs (the game chdir()s
there at startup) -- see sdl_tile_candidates in win/sdl/sdlmain.c.
`make install` copies tilesets/unchozo32b.png and tilesets/anim/*.png
into HACKDIR automatically. If the sheet is missing, the port still
runs -- it just draws colored characters instead of tiles.

Known gaps (intentionally deferred, not bugs)
-----------------------------------------------
  - No real role/race/gender/alignment selection dialog
    (sdl_player_selection() is a no-op; unset facets fall back to the
    game's normal "pick at random" behavior). See
    win/tty/wintty.c:tty_player_selection for what a full dialog looks
    like.
  - No persistent inventory window (WC_PERM_INVENT not advertised).
  - No message history scrollback viewer (sdl_doprev_message() is a
    stub) -- full history is kept internally, just not wired to a key.
  - Font/DPI handling is minimal: one fixed point size, no HiDPI
    scaling. Tile art always renders at native tile resolution (32x32
    for unchozo32b.png), no scaling either.

(Closing the window now saves and exits via hangup(), like a tty
session losing its terminal, and '#' extended commands work via a
typed prompt with prefix matching -- both were gaps earlier.)

Animated tile overlays
----------------------
Drop PNG strips into HACKDIR/anim/ named by tile number, e.g.
anim/1021.png for tile 1021 (the fountain). Each file is a horizontal
strip of square frames -- frame size is the image height, so any
resolution works (128x128 frames in a 512x128 strip = 4 HD frames) and
gets smoothly scaled into the tile cell. Tiles without an overlay keep
their static sheet art, so animated art can be added one piece at a
time. All copies of a tile animate in sync at 200ms/frame off the wall
clock, independent of game turns.

The art pipeline (e.g. Meshy or any tool that exports PNG frames):

1. Find the tile number: grep tilesets/TILE-INDEX.txt (generated from
   the game's own glyph2tile[] table -- never derive indices by
   counting the win/share/*.txt files, that gives wrong answers).
       grep -i kitten tilesets/TILE-INDEX.txt
2. Export animation frames as PNGs with transparent backgrounds
   (any size; they get alpha-trimmed, squared, and bottom-aligned).
3. Stitch them into a strip (accepts a name or a tile number):
       tools/.venv/bin/python tools/mkanim.py kitten frames_dir/
   One-time setup for the venv:
       python3 -m venv tools/.venv
       tools/.venv/bin/pip install pillow
4. Deploy and play:
       make install && graphichack

Demo overlays live in tilesets/anim/ (fountain 1021, dog family
18-20, cat family 37/38/42).
